t structures. M3/M5 are synergistic with M1 (thick electrodes dilute current density) but M2/M4 are the antagonistic pair. Expected Pareto negotiation: increase electrode thickness moderately, recover transport losses via electrolyte formulation (σ/D overrides = low-freezing, high-conductivity electrolyte) and small negative particle radius, then tune SEI kinetics to close M5.\n\n**Rough ceiling estimates (from parameter dump, to be confirmed by simulation):** mass of active layers+collectors+separator at baseline ≈ 0.0435 kg; 5 Ah × ~3.6 V ≈ 18 Wh → ED ≈ 410 Wh/kg — M1 likely passable at baseline with margin; the binding constraints are expected to be M4 (Nyman2008 σ(c,T) collapses at 253.15 K) and M5 (500-cycle SEI growth), plus M2 (4C = 20 A on this geometry).\n\n## 2. Candidate strategy\n\n- **Round 1 (baseline characterization):** run Chen2020 defaults through all five protocols: 1C_discharge (SPMe) → calc-energy; 4C_charge_45C (lumped+plating); lowT_discharge; aging 100 cyc; aging 500 cyc. Opening ceiling assessment from these numbers.\n- **Rounds 2–n (architecture + formulation, 2–4 variants/round per exploration_force):** variants in the parameter space that the failure signature indicates:\n  - M4 failure → electrolyte formulation override (constant `Electrolyte conductivity [S.m-1]` ≈ 1.0–1.5 S/m, `Electrolyte diffusivity [m2.s-1]` ≈ 3–5e-10, physical interpretation: low-freezing/high-transport electrolyte for cold-climate grid duty) + smaller positive/negative particle radius + thinner electrodes.\n  - M2 failure → negative particle radius ↓ (5.86 → ~2–3 µm), N/P ↑ (thicker negative), separator thinner, σ↑.\n  - M3/M5 failure → `SEI kinetic rate constant [m.s-1]` ↓ (coating, 1–2 orders), `SEI reaction exchange current density [A.m-2]` ↓, thicker electrodes (↓ areal current density).\n  - M1 failure (if ED < 327.18) → thicken electrodes, thin collectors/separator, lower porosity — then re-verify M2/M4.\n- **Precision escalation:** SPMe screens all variants; DFN re-runs only for near-passing candidates (plating and low-T are transport-limited — DFN confirmation before acceptance).\n- **Ceiling escalation (on):** if after opening assessment the architecture+formulation ceiling provably cannot close a gap, escalate to Stage 2 material design (electrolyte additive invention via molecular funnel, or system switch to OKane2022 which carries a cracking model and SiOx-capacity upside — checked against aging capability).\n\n## 3. Budget allocation\n\n- Baseline characterization: 6 runs (seconds–minutes each).\n- Architecture/formulation optimization: up to ~8 rounds × 3 variants; SPMe screening (seconds) with DFN confirmation for finalists (~2–4 DFN runs); aging 500-cycle runs only for candidates whose 100-cycle SEI trajectory extrapolates ≤ 550 nm (each run ≈ minutes).\n- Total simulation budget ≈ 30–40 runs; no true compute (real_compute=false).\n\n## 4. Risk and fallback plan\n\n- **R1: M4 (−20°C ≥ 90%) hard-limited by solid-phase diffusion/kinetics** (forbidden lever: solid diffusivity/conductivity). Fallback sequence: electrolyte σ/D ↑ → particle radius ↓ → electrode thickness/porosity ↓ → N/P tuning. If still short → three-strike questioning of the boundary (is a constant-σ \"low-freezing electrolyte\" a legitimate formulation design within scope? yes — electrolyte formulation is an adjustable degree of freedom, widest interpretation recorded in entry 0).\n- **R2: M5 (SEI@500 ≤ 550) vs SEI model linearity.** If SEI grows near-linearly with cycles, a 500-cycle pass forces aggressive kinetic suppression — fallback to SEI-kinetic coating (×0.1, ×0.01) plus thick-electrode current-density dilution; verify on 100-cycle first (cheap) before committing a 500-cycle run.\n- **R3: M2 (4C plating) at high N/P conflicts with M1.** Managed via particle-size + electrolyte levers first (mass-neutral), geometry second.\n- **R4: SPMe/DFN divergence** on transport-limited protocols (M2/M4) — always confirm finalists in DFN; record model_used honestly.\n- **Three-strike rule:** any single failure cause surviving 3 consecutive fix attempts triggers layer-by-layer questioning (system assumption → task-boundary assumption → metric assumption), recorded in a plan-update entry, then direction change or honest negative-result close.\n\n## 5. References (directional basis)\n\n- Low-temperature electrolyte transport limits / low-freezing formulations → Smart, Ratnakumar, Surampudi, *J. Electrochem. Soc.* 149 (2002) A361 (low-temperature electrolyte survey); Nyman, Behm, Lindbergh, *Electrochim. Acta* 53 (2008) 6356 (LiPF6 EC/EMC transport — the Chen2020 set's σ/D source).\n- Lithium plating onset criterion (negative electrode potential vs Li/Li⁺ < 0 V) → Waldmann et al., *J. Power Sources* 384 (2018) 107 (plating review).\n- SEI growth kinetics (ec-reaction-limited, coating suppression) → Peled, *J. Electrochem. Soc.* 126 (1979) 2047 (SEI concept); Chen et al., *J. Electrochem. Soc.* 167 (2020) 080534 (the Chen2020 parameterization, incl. SEI kinetics).\n- Particle-size effect on rate capability/plating resistance → domain experience (small particle → higher active surface, lower local current density); no precise source.\n- Grid-storage chemistry trade-offs (NMC energy vs LFP cycle life) → Padhi, Nanjundaswamy, Goodenough, *J. Electrochem. Soc.* 144 (1997) 1188 (LFP); LFP ED ceiling below 327.18 Wh/kg in this parameter library — domain experience (no precise source).\n\n## Revision history\n- v1 (2026-09-09): initial plan.\n"}
</tool_use>
